      'Start with the shirt type. A button-down work shirt with pockets suits techs and installers, a polo reads more polished on client visits, and a tee is the low-cost option for warehouse and event days. Browse <a href="/catalog/work-shirts/">work shirts in the catalog</a>. Direct embroidery looks cleanest for logos and names, while a sewn patch lets you reuse the shirt when someone leaves; <a href="/embroidery-vs-patches-uniforms/">embroidery vs patches for uniforms</a> compares the two. Weighing a rental program instead? See <a href="/cintas-unifirst-aramark-alternatives-las-vegas/">owning vs renting uniforms</a>, and for Nevada crews, <a href="/custom-work-shirts-las-vegas/">custom work shirts in Las Vegas</a>. We price each role on its own line, so changing the warehouse shirt leaves the office polos alone.',
      'Uniforms drift when each location buys on its own. One office orders a slightly different navy, another shrinks the logo, and within a year nothing matches. The fix is one page in a shared folder: garment style and color, logo file, thread colors, placement, and who may reorder.',
